    vitals: "HR: 145 bpm, BP: 78/52 mmHg, RR: 39 breaths/min, Temp: 39.5°C, SpO2: 91%",
    labs: "Wells Score for DVT: 3 (likely DVT). Wells Score for PE: 6 (high probability PE). D-dimer: elevated 3.2 mcg/mL (should not be used for diagnosis in surgical patients — too non-specific). ABG: pH 7.45, pCO2 32, pO2 68 on RA, HCO3 24. Troponin: 0.04 ng/mL (high-sensitivity, normal). ECG: sinus tachycardia, no S1Q3T3. CXR: normal.",
    imaging: "CT Pulmonary Angiogram: Intraluminal filling defect in right lower lobe segmental pulmonary artery. Left lower limb venous Doppler: Acute DVT in left femoral and popliteal veins (non-occlusive thrombus).",
    diagnosis: "Acute Left Lower Extremity Deep Vein Thrombosis with Ipsilateral Segmental Pulmonary Embolism (Provoked by Surgery)",
    ddx: ["PE with DVT", "Community-Acquired Pneumonia", "Acute Coronary Syndrome", "Musculoskeletal Chest Pain", "Wound Infection"],
    goals: "Treat VTE, prevent thrombus extension and recurrence, reduce VTE-related mortality and morbidity, prevent post-thrombotic syndrome",
    pharm: "1. Anticoagulation: Start LMWH (Enoxaparin 1.5mg/kg SC daily or 1mg/kg SC BID) — dose: 100mg daily for a 67kg patient. Bridge to oral anticoagulant. 2. Long-term anticoagulation: Warfarin (target INR 2-3, starting day 1 of LMWH, continue LMWH for >5 days and until INR >2 for 24 hours). OR DOAC: Rivaroxaban 15mg BID for 21 days, then 20mg daily, OR Apixaban 10mg BID for 7 days then 5mg BID. 3. DOAC is preferred over warfarin due to no monitoring requirement and fewer drug interactions. 4. Thrombolysis (systemic or catheter-directed): indicated for massive PE with haemodynamic instability (not indicated here — normotensive). 5. Compression stockings: to prevent post-thrombotic syndrome. 6. Ambulation as tolerated (no evidence that bedrest reduces embolisation).",
    nonPharm: "Ambulation with compression stockings. Leg elevation when sitting. Incentive spirometry. DVT prophylaxis counselling for future surgeries.",
    carePlan: "Admit to medical ward. Start therapeutic LMWH immediately (before CT results if high suspicion). Start Rivaroxaban 15mg BID. Discharge when stable (afebrile, haemodynamically stable, oxygenating well on room air). Duration: minimum 3-6 months. Assess for provoked vs unprovoked aetiology.",
    dtps: "1. Acute proximal DVT with segmental PE requiring full therapeutic anticoagulation. 2. Provoked by surgery and temporary risk factor — determine optimal treatment duration.",
    monitoring: "Daily: vitals, SpO2, leg circumference. Check for bleeding (any anticoagulant). If on warfarin: INR daily until therapeutic. DOAC: no routine monitoring. After discharge: review in 2 weeks for symptom assessment.",
    counselling: "Blood clots after surgery are a recognised complication. Treatment involves blood thinning medications for at least 3-6 months. Rivaroxaban is once-daily after the first 3 weeks. Watch for signs of bleeding: unusual bruising, blood in urine/stool, bleeding gums. Seek immediate help if you develop severe headache (rule out intracranial bleed). Wear compression stockings to reduce leg swelling long-term.",
    followUp: "Haematology/GP in 2 weeks. Anticoagulation clinic (if on warfarin). Duration: at least 3 months. If unprovoked or recurrent, consider extended anticoagulation (>12 months). Review bleeding risk (HAS-BLED score) at each visit.",
    pearls: "VTE management has 3 phases: acute (first 5-10 days), long-term (3-6 months), and extended (beyond 6 months if high risk of recurrence). DOACs (Rivaroxaban, Apixaban, Edoxaban, Dabigatran) have largely replaced warfarin in Kenya for eligible patients (available through KEMSA and private pharmacies). Warfarin remains the standard for mechanical heart valves and antiphospholipid syndrome. LMWH is preferred for acute phase in cancer-associated thrombosis. Compression stockings reduce post-thrombotic syndrome by 50%.",
    references: ["CHEST Guidelines: VTE Management", "ESC Guidelines: PE Management", "Kenya Clinical Guidelines: Thromboembolism"],
