    pharm: "1. HOLD Warfarin immediately — do not take tonight's dose. 2. Vitamin K decision: INR 5.8 WITHOUT bleeding — observation alone is acceptable if INR expected to correct within 24-48 hours. However, given mechanical mitral valve (highest thrombotic risk valve position), consider LOW-DOSE oral Vitamin K (1-2.5mg PO) to accelerate correction while maintaining some anticoagulation. Avoid high-dose Vitamin K (over-correction risks thrombosis). 3. Re-check INR in 12-24 hours. 4. If INR drops to <5.0 by next day: resume Warfarin at REDUCED dose (e.g., 3.5-4mg daily instead of 5mg). 5. Address drug interaction: Amoxicillin inhibits gut flora that produce vitamin K, increases warfarin effect. Options: switch to non-interacting antibiotic (Azithromycin 500mg OD for dental abscess) OR reduce warfarin dose during antibiotic course. 6. Address green tea: Vitamin K in green tea — patient now DRINKING it (was not before), which would normally DECREASE INR. But she started it recently and it has not yet reached steady state. The antibiotic effect is currently dominant. 7. Paracetamol: high-dose paracetamol (>2g/day for >3 days) can increase INR — reduce to 500mg QID or 1g TID (max 3g/day). 8. Once INR stabilised: resume Warfarin at adjusted dose, re-check INR weekly until stable, then return to routine monitoring.",
    nonPharm: "Counselling completed on drug interactions: antibiotics (especially amoxicillin, metronidazole, ciprofloxacin, co-trimoxazole), herbal products (green tea, cranberry juice, St. John's Wort), dietary vitamin K consistency, and paracetamol dose. Provided written drug interaction card. Discussed when to seek emergency care (signs of bleeding). INR monitoring schedule reinforced.",
    carePlan: "1. INR 5.8 (critically elevated) — 2.3x upper limit of target range. 2. Drug-drug interaction identified as precipitant (amoxicillin + warfarin). 3. Mechanical mitral valve carries highest thrombotic risk — must maintain adequate anticoagulation.",
    dtps: "Re-check INR in 12-24 hours (today at 4pm, tomorrow morning). If INR <5: resume warfarin at reduced dose and re-check in 3 days. If INR still >5: repeat low-dose Vitamin K and re-check. Weekly INR until stable (2 consecutive INRs in range). Then return to monthly monitoring. Document interaction in patient records. Flag amoxicillin as interacting antibiotic.",
    monitoring: "Your blood thinner level is too high — this increases your bleeding risk. We are holding your warfarin for tonight and will recheck your blood tomorrow. The cause is the antibiotic (amoxicillin) your dentist prescribed — it interacts with warfarin and makes it stronger. Always tell any doctor, dentist, or pharmacist that you take warfarin before they prescribe ANY medication. Some antibiotics, painkillers, and herbal products can dangerously affect your warfarin level. Keep your vitamin K intake consistent — do not suddenly start or stop eating green leafy vegetables or drinking green tea. Seek emergency care immediately if you notice: blood in urine or stool, severe headache, vomiting blood, or unusual bruising.",
    counselling: "INR tomorrow morning. Anticoagulation clinic weekly until stable (2 consecutive INRs in range). Gastroenterology referral if bleeding develops. Cardiology review to confirm target INR range. Dental follow-up for abscess management. Consider INR self-management education (patient already self-managing).",
    followUp: "Warfarin has a narrow therapeutic index and is subject to numerous drug interactions. Amoxicillin increases INR by inhibiting vitamin K-producing gut bacteria — this interaction is well-documented and can raise INR by 0.5-2.0 units. Mechanical mitral valves require the highest INR target (2.5-3.5) and carry the greatest thrombotic risk if under-anticoagulated. Low-dose Vitamin K (1-2.5mg) is preferred for supratherapeutic INR without bleeding — it reduces INR without fully reversing anticoagulation. High-dose Vitamin K (>5mg) risks over-correction and thrombosis in mechanical valve patients. In Kenya, anticoagulation clinic services are limited — patient self-management with point-of-care INR devices is an emerging model.",
    pearls: "Warfarin is considered one of the most dangerous medications when not properly monitored due to its narrow therapeutic index. Always maintain consistent vitamin K intake. Drug interactions with warfarin are numerous — always check before adding any new medication. Amoxicillin is a commonly overlooked interacting drug.",
    references: ["ACCP Guidelines: Warfarin Management", "ESC Guidelines: Mechanical Heart Valve Anticoagulation", "Kenya Clinical Guidelines: Anticoagulation", "WHO: Patient Safety — Medication Interactions"],
